WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.375
About 20 new

00:00:01.187 --> 00:00:02.684
Rust GUI toolkits

00:00:02.543 --> 00:00:04.005
since 2022

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:05.756
Raph Levien has

00:00:05.568 --> 00:00:06.358
followed up his

00:00:06.169 --> 00:00:06.935
advice for the

00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:07.820
next dozen Rust

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:08.492
GUIs

00:00:08.622 --> 00:00:09.730
written four and

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.367
a half years

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:10.721
ago

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.333
Roughly 20 toolkits

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.041
have been announced

00:00:12.853 --> 00:00:13.560
since

00:00:14.086 --> 00:00:15.135
He argues Rust

00:00:14.947 --> 00:00:15.984
UI is still

00:00:15.796 --> 00:00:16.527
compelling

00:00:16.551 --> 00:00:17.824
cross-platform apps

00:00:17.636 --> 00:00:18.767
are often slow

00:00:18.921 --> 00:00:20.288
platform toolkits have

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:20.961
stagnated

00:00:21.173 --> 00:00:22.659
Electron keeps spreading

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.519
and RAM is

00:00:23.331 --> 00:00:24.227
expensive

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:26.031
Few flagship apps

00:00:25.843 --> 00:00:26.609
use Rust for

00:00:26.421 --> 00:00:27.246
UI yet

00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:28.555
but Zed's GPUI

00:00:28.366 --> 00:00:29.416
proves it ships

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:30.147
and it is

00:00:29.958 --> 00:00:30.878
emerging as a

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.715
top choice for

00:00:31.574 --> 00:00:32.800
AI-driven rewrites

00:00:32.848 --> 00:00:34.215
notably at Discord

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:35.748
Full story on

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:36.455
the dashboard
